// RefundSignatureRequest A refund request names the payment and proves which quote it was awarded under.
// Everything else veri-fill needs it reads for itself.
//
// WHY SO FEW FIELDS. The witness members the escrow checks -- depositor, token,
// amount, feeAmount, reserver, releaser, settler and the two commitments -- are all
// recorded on chain by the deposit. veri-fill reads them from the Deposited and
// DepositCommitments events and rebuilds the ActiveDeposit tuple, then requires its
// keccak256 to equal what the escrow stored. That is the same check Escrow._refund
// applies, so a witness that passes here cannot revert InvalidWitnessValues.
//
// Taking those members from the caller instead would mean signing values nobody had
// verified, and it made the endpoint unusable by an originator, who knows none of
// them. The escrow address is read from veri-fill's own per-chain configuration for
// the same reason: it is the EIP-712 verifyingContract, so a caller who could choose
// it could choose which contract the signature works against.
//
// CANCEL EVIDENCE, for an originator fulfill only. When the quote's payment request
// names the originator as the fulfill executor, delivery is the settler's EIP-3009
// authorization on the destination token, nonce the quote hash. Without
// `authorization_canceled`, veri-fill refunds only after `fulfillment_deadline`, when the
// token's `authorizationState` shows the authorization was never used or canceled; a
// canceled one reads the same as a used one and is refused. With it, veri-fill reads that
// one receipt and requires the log at `log_index` to be the destination token's
// `AuthorizationCanceled(authorizer, nonce)` for the quote's settler and quote hash, in a
// successful, finalized transaction. A cancel means the authorization can never be used,
// so the refund is then signed at any time, before or after the deadline. A locator that
// does not resolve to that log is refused; it cannot make veri-fill sign anything it would
// otherwise decline. veri-fill never scans for the event. It is ignored for a settler
// fulfill, and without `fulfillment_quote`, since the settler and quote hash it is matched
// against come from the quote.
//
// THE QUOTE OR THE PAYER'S REQUEST. A request sends `fulfillment_quote`,
// `payment_request_json`, or both, and a request with neither is refused. veri-fill
// enforces this rule, not the schema, so generated clients type both fields as
// optional, as on the release request.
type RefundSignatureRequest struct {
	// AuthorizationCanceled Points at one log. `log_index` is the receipt `logIndex` (index in the block).
	AuthorizationCanceled *OnchainLogLocator `json:"authorization_canceled,omitempty"`

	// DepositTransactionHash OPTIONAL locator for the transaction that created the deposit. A hint, never a
	// fact: supplying it lets veri-fill read one receipt instead of scanning a block
	// range, and a wrong value simply finds no matching deposit and is refused. It
	// cannot make veri-fill sign anything it would otherwise decline.
	DepositTransactionHash *string `json:"deposit_transaction_hash,omitempty"`

	// FulfillmentQuote The settler-signed FulfillmentQuote for this payment, as the signed document
	// itself. Same shape and semantics as the `fulfillment_quote` returned by
	// the payment gateway's award and status responses, so a caller forwards that value
	// straight through.
	//
	// FORWARD THE VALUES UNCHANGED. quote_hash is derived by canonicalizing this
	// document, so re-ordering keys or re-indenting is harmless -- canonicalization
	// removes both. What is NOT harmless is changing any VALUE: renormalizing a
	// timestamp (`...T00:00:00.000Z` to `...T00:00:00Z`) or re-serializing the
	// embedded `payment_request_json`, which is a string field and is hashed
	// literally. Either yields a different hash and the request is refused.
	//
	// It is verified against the chain, not trusted: veri-fill computes its hash and
	// requires that to equal the quoteHash the escrow recorded for this payment at
	// deposit. That anchor is what lets the quote supply the deadlines and the
	// corridor without the caller being able to choose them.
	//
	// A settler has it from the award it acted on. An originator has it from the quote
	// batch it awarded from, or from the payment gateway's synchronous payment response.
	//
	// GET /v1/payments/{paymentId}/status can also return it, but the gateway ships with
	// that field gated off, so an originator flow that depends on the status endpoint
	// depends on that deployment opting in first.
	//
	// Required unless `payment_request_json` is sent, and always for an originator
	// fulfill: its refund needs the quote's `settler_address`, which signed the
	// authorization. veri-fill also reads an explicit null as absent, though the
	// schema does not declare the field nullable.
	FulfillmentQuote json.RawMessage `json:"fulfillment_quote,omitempty"`

	// PaymentId The escrow deposit identifier, as a 0x-prefixed 32-byte hex string. This is the
	// escrow's `depositId`, so it is what `getDepositHash` is keyed on.
	PaymentId string `json:"payment_id"`

	// PaymentRequestJson The payment request the payer signed, as its raw JSON text. A string, not an
	// object, for the same reason as FulfillmentQuote.payment_request_json: it is read
	// as received, so nothing re-serializes it on the way.
	//
	// It is read only when the deposit commits depositRequestHash v2 and the hash of
	// this request's terms equals it, and only when its destination also matches the
	// deposit's destination commitment. The quote hash is then read from the deposit,
	// and only the members v2 commits are read from this copy. When `fulfillment_quote`
	// is also sent, a copy that does not match is ignored and the refund proceeds from
	// the quote as before, so a v1 deposit sent with both is never refused for it. Only a
	// request carrying `payment_request_json` alone, without a match, is refused.
	//
	// This lets a refund proceed for a settler fulfill when the settler withholds or
	// corrupts its quote, and serves an unreserved Solana deposit, whose quote hash is
	// zero and which has no quote to send. An originator fulfill still needs
	// `fulfillment_quote` (see above).
	PaymentRequestJson *string `json:"payment_request_json,omitempty"`

	// ReserveLocation OPTIONAL. Where the reserve of this payment's deposit landed: for a direct deposit, the
	// settler's own reserve, which it sends from the reserve it recorded. It is not what
	// `deposit_transaction_hash` locates: that is the transaction that created the deposit
	// (for a direct deposit, the payer's own). The three members travel together.
	//
	// A HINT, NEVER A FACT. Absent or wrong, the gate answers what it would have without it
	// once its node has reached `block_number`. It cannot make veri-fill sign anything it would
	// otherwise decline.
	//
	// HOW IT IS READ. veri-fill does not rely on the transaction or on a log index, because
	// a settler may batch reserves and one transaction can then hold several
	// `DepositReserved` logs. It asks its own node for the logs at `block_hash`, filtered by
	// the Escrow it has configured for that chain, by topic0
	// `DepositReserved(bytes32 indexed depositId, bytes32 quoteHash, address settler, uint256 amount, uint256 feeAmount)`
	// and by this payment's deposit id as the indexed `depositId`, so other reserves in the
	// same batch cannot be mistaken for this one.
	//
	// A log for this deposit id at `block_hash` is the reserve whichever transaction emitted it,
	// so `transaction_hash` is not part of the match. `reserve()` has no caller check (it is
	// authorised by the reserver's signature), so someone else can submit the settler's signed
	// reserve first and the settler's own transaction then reverts; the deposit is reserved all
	// the same. veri-fill records a `transaction_hash` that differs from the log's, ignoring
	// case and the `0x` prefix, and does not act on it.
	//
	// veri-fill reads the location only when the escrow's stored hash still reads this deposit
	// as unreserved, which is after the settled-history checks (never deposited, released,
	// refunded), so a failing read of the location cannot hold a payment that history already
	// settles.
	//
	// - If the node's head is below `block_number`, the node has not caught up with the
	//   reserve, and the refund answers 503 (retry) rather than a refusal.
	// - If the head is at or above `block_number`, veri-fill also reads the block at that
	//   number. It ignores the hint when the node positively reports a different hash
	//   there (a reorg or a wrong hash), or reports that same hash there and the log query at
	//   `block_hash`, answered for that same block, returns no log for this deposit id (a
	//   wrong id). An error, or an unknown block or hash, is not evidence that the block or
	//   the log is absent, and neither is a `[]` that cannot be tied to the block the node
	//   confirmed: the refund answers 503 (retry).
	// - A `block_number` too far above the node's head is ignored. veri-fill sets that cap.
	// - A `block_number` far below the node's head is ignored as well. The hint exists for a
	//   node that lags just after the reserve, and a node that is long past the block is not
	//   lagging, so a block it can no longer serve does not hold the refund at 503. veri-fill
	//   sets that distance too.
	// - Send it only when the source chain is `eip155:*`. `block_hash` is an EVM hash, so a
	//   location of another family fails validation. veri-fill ignores a well-formed one on a
	//   source chain that is not `eip155:*`.
	ReserveLocation *ReserveLocation `json:"reserve_location,omitempty"`

	// SettlementReport The awarded settler's signed statement that it gave up on fulfilling this payment: a
	// SettlementResult declaration with phase FULFILLMENT and outcome ERROR, and the
	// SettlementResultEnvelope holder VP whose proof.challenge is that declaration's content
	// hash. Both members are the verbatim strings the settler sends on the settler session
	// stream, so any holder of the report forwards them unchanged.
	//
	// A verified report removes the fulfillment deadline wait and the destination scan, so the
	// refund is signed as soon as the deposit is live and anchored to the quote. veri-fill
	// accepts it when the VP verifies for this environment's domain, the holder is the signer of
	// `fulfillment_quote`, and the declared quote_hash (and, outside Solana, payment_id) match
	// this request. It is ignored when the quote names the originator as the fulfill executor.
	//
	// AN UNVERIFIABLE REPORT IS NEVER A REFUSAL. A report that fails any check is treated
	// exactly as an absent one, so the request is answered by the normal deadline path.
	SettlementReport *SettlementReport `json:"settlement_report,omitempty"`
}

// RefundSignatureResponse defines model for RefundSignatureResponse.
type RefundSignatureResponse struct {
	// DepositVerdict A signed attestation of what this service observed about a payment's escrow deposit
	// on the SOURCE chain, so a consumer can act on the signature instead of repeating the
	// chain read.
	//
	// The counterpart to `FulfillmentVerdict` and NOT a variant of it. That object attests
	// the destination chain: its `chain_id` names the destination, selects the destination
	// key, and its block members describe a destination block. The escrow lives on the
	// source chain, and in a cross-chain-family corridor the two chains do not even share a
	// signature scheme. One object cannot attest a fact about one chain while its block and
	// its key name another, so this is a separate attestation with its own chain member,
	// its own block, its own outcome set and its own signing tag.
	//
	// Also distinct from the escrow refund witness that may accompany it. That witness is
	// consumed by the escrow contract, binds escrow members, carries neither a block nor a
	// transaction, and its typehash is fixed by deployed contracts. This object attests an
	// observation and is consumed offchain only. Two signatures over two structs, and
	// neither substitutes for the other.
	//
	// ABSENCE IS NOT AN OUTCOME. A response with no `deposit_verdict` says nothing about
	// the deposit. A consumer MUST NOT read a missing verdict as `not_held`, and MUST fall
	// back to the root members of the response carrying it.
	//
	// PRECEDENCE, AND ONLY ONCE VERIFIED. A verdict is attacker-supplied data until its
	// signature checks out, so presence alone confers nothing. A consumer MUST complete
	// every step of HOW A THIRD PARTY VERIFIES below, and a verdict that fails any step
	// MUST be treated as ABSENT rather than as a negative finding.
	//
	// SIGNING SCHEME (normative). `signature` covers the members of this object, and what
	// it is computed over is DERIVED from them and never carried on the wire. The form
	// follows the chain family, as `signature_scheme` names it.
	//
	// On `eip155:*` (`eip712-secp256k1`) it is EIP-712 typed data, with the domain given on
	// `VerdictSignatureScheme` and the primary type
	//
	//   `DepositVerdict(string domain,bytes32 paymentId,string chainId,address escrowAddress,string outcome,uint256 blockNumber,bytes32 blockHash,string finalityPolicy,bytes32 transactionHash)`
	//
	// Each field carries the member of the same meaning verbatim: `chainId` is the whole
	// CAIP-2 `chain_id` string and `blockNumber` the decimal `block_number`. An empty
	// `transaction_hash` is encoded as the zero bytes32. That is unambiguous because the
	// member is populated exactly when `outcome` is `held`, and a producer never spells
	// the zero value explicitly. A consumer MUST reject an eip155 verdict whose
	// `transaction_hash` spells the zero hash, since it signs the same digest as an empty
	// one.
	//
	// On `tron:*` and `solana:*` it is a preimage on the same framing as
	// `FulfillmentVerdict`: UTF-8, one LF (0x0A) terminating every line including the last,
	// no 0x0D, `<n>` the length in bytes of the value that follows in base-10 with no
	// leading zeros, and `<n>:` present even when the value is empty. See that object for
	// why the framing is injective and why every value is printable ASCII.
	//
	// The preimage is exactly TEN lines, in this fixed order and no other:
	//
	//   1. the literal `atum-verdict/deposit/1`
	//   2. `domain=<n>:<domain>`
	//   3. `payment_id=<n>:<payment_id>`
	//   4. `chain_id=<n>:<chain_id>`
	//   5. `escrow_address=<n>:<escrow_address>`
	//   6. `outcome=<n>:<outcome>`
	//   7. `block_number=<n>:<block_number>`
	//   8. `block_hash=<n>:<block_hash>`
	//   9. `finality_policy=<n>:<finality_policy>`
	//  10. `transaction_hash=<n>:<transaction_hash>`
	//
	// Both forms name the attestation kind, the typed data by its primary type and the
	// preimage by its first line, which is what stops a signature over a fulfillment
	// verdict satisfying a verifier of this one. Both kinds can be signed by the same key
	// wherever a chain is both source and destination, and the kind is the only thing
	// separating their signing domains.
	//
	// HOW A THIRD PARTY VERIFIES, in order, failing closed at the first step that does not
	// hold:
	//
	//   1. `domain` is `did:web:` followed by the lowercased host, without a port, of the
	//      fulfillment verifier endpoint the payment names, and not merely a well-formed one.
	//   2. `payment_id` is the payment being asked about. A signature proves what was
	//      attested, never which question it answers, so a genuine verdict for another
	//      payment passes every remaining step.
	//   3. `chain_id` is the SOURCE chain of the payment being asked about, not the
	//      destination chain and not whatever the verdict was handed carrying.
	//   4. `escrow_address` is the escrow the payment's deposit was made into. Without this
	//      step a `not_held` verdict read against the wrong contract is indistinguishable
	//      from a correct one.
	//   5. `signature_scheme` is the one scheme that chain admits, and `signature` has the
	//      length that scheme requires.
	//   6. `signature` recovers to the payment's `fulfillment_verifier.account`, the escrow
	//      releaser its deposit names. After a key rotation that may be a key the fulfillment
	//      verifier endpoint no longer publishes.
	//   7. Only then read `outcome`, and switch on it rather than negating it.
	//
	// Anyone with chain access can falsify the claim afterwards without trusting this
	// service: call the escrow's deposit getter for `payment_id` at `block_number`, and
	// check that the chain still reports `block_hash` at that height. A verdict describing
	// a block that is no longer canonical is void, and a void verdict is not a finding.
	DepositVerdict *DepositVerdict `json:"deposit_verdict,omitempty"`

	// Details Detailed error message if signature generation failed
	Details *string `json:"details,omitempty"`

	// Error Error code if signature generation failed.
	//
	// Bound to `deposit_verdict` and `fulfillment_verdict` when either is present. All
	// four refusal codes below are TERMINAL as refusals: none is a reason to retry. The
	// code itself is not final: a later ask can report a different one of the four as the
	// deposit settles or its history moves out of reach.
	//
	// `fulfillment_verdict` attests the DESTINATION chain and is signed by the key
	// served for its `chain_id`, never the source key behind the root `signature`. On
	// this route its pairing is the inverse of the release response:
	//
	// - `outcome: not_delivered` accompanies `verified: true` and the refund
	//   `signature`, and names the final destination block the absence was proven to.
	// - `outcome: delivered` accompanies `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS`, and accompanies
	//   `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_RELEASED` whenever a destination scan found the delivery, however
	//   the release itself was established. The same code also appears WITHOUT a verdict
	//   when no scan found one. A delivered verdict does not imply the delivery was on time. The release
	//   route never refuses a delivery for being late, so on `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS`, where
	//   the escrow still holds the deposit, the delivery named here is not stranded by its
	//   timing.
	// - It is absent on every other answer, and on destinations this service cannot
	//   attest yet (today only `eip155:*` destinations carry one). ABSENCE IS NOT AN
	//   OUTCOME: a consumer MUST NOT read a missing verdict as either finding.
	//
	// A consumer MUST run every step of HOW A THIRD PARTY VERIFIES on
	// `FulfillmentVerdict`, binding it to the payment and to the destination chain its
	// own quote names, before acting on it.
	//
	// `deposit_verdict` accompanies `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS` and `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_RELEASED`
	// when the deposit was found, with `outcome: held` anchored at the block its
	// transaction was mined in. Today only `eip155:*` sources carry one. The deposit is found
	// from `deposit_transaction_hash`, or from the escrow's own deposit events near the
	// chain head. It is signed by the payment's `fulfillment_verifier.account`, the escrow
	// releaser on the SOURCE chain. It is what lets a
	// consumer that never saw the deposit record it, and it is absent on every other
	// answer.
	//
	// `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS` reports that the payment WAS delivered on its destination
	// chain, so a refund would pay twice. The escrow may still hold the deposit, which
	// is exactly what a delivered-but-not-yet-released payment looks like, so a
	// consumer MUST NOT infer from a live deposit that a refund is owed. It is also the
	// answer when veri-fill already signed this payment's release and the delivery that
	// release was granted on is still final on its destination chain.
	//
	// `RELEASE_ALREADY_SIGNED` reports that veri-fill already signed this payment's
	// release, and that the delivery it was granted on could NOT be confirmed on its
	// destination chain now: it was not found final there, or that chain cannot be
	// re-read by this service. A signed verdict is final, so no refund will be signed,
	// and veri-fill pages a person on it. It does NOT say the payment was delivered, and
	// it does not say where the deposit is: the release may or may not have been
	// submitted yet.
	//
	// `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_REFUNDED` and `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_RELEASED` report a settled
	// history read from the escrow's own events, on `eip155:*`, `tron:*` and `solana:*`
	// sources alike. When the escrow no longer holds the deposit, veri-fill first anchors
	// the deposit: from `deposit_transaction_hash`, or from the escrow's deposit events in
	// the most recent blocks on `eip155:*` and `tron:*`, or from the deposit account's
	// transaction history on `solana:*`. On `eip155:*` and `tron:*` a deposit recorded
	// under another quote is refused before any settlement is read; on `solana:*` the
	// quote is checked against the settlement event's own when the event carries one.
	// The escrow's `Refunded` and `Released` events, and `RefundedMany` and
	// `ReleasedMany` on `eip155:*` and `tron:*`, are then scanned from the deposit
	// onwards, however far back it lies. With no deposit anchored, the same recent
	// blocks are still searched for a settlement of this payment, and a single
	// settlement found there carries no quote to check, so neither code proves the
	// caller's quote matched. A refund read this way carries its transaction in
	// `refund_transaction_hash`.
	//
	// Both codes, and `INVALID_REQUEST`, are answered as soon as they are known, before
	// the fulfillment deadline as well as after it. A history that cannot be read yet,
	// because a read failed or the scan did not finish, answers 503, never a refusal.
	//
	// When the escrow's events cannot say and the deposit is anchored on an `eip155:*`
	// or `tron:*` source, the answer after the deadline comes from a scan of the
	// destination chain instead: `..._RELEASED` when it finds a delivery,
	// `..._REFUNDED` when it finds none in the finalized history. A scan answer is a
	// reading of the destination, not of the escrow: a delivery that has not finalized
	// yet reads as none, and a delivery that landed after a refund reads as released.
	// `..._RELEASED` means the recipient was paid.
	//
	// BOTH ARE BEST-EFFORT, AND THEIR ABSENCE PROVES NOTHING. A consumer MUST NOT assume
	// a released payment always reports as released. Unless the destination chain
	// resolves it as above, two gaps produce `DEPOSIT_NOT_HELD` for a payment that did in
	// fact settle:
	//
	// - Without `deposit_transaction_hash`, an `eip155:*` or `tron:*` deposit is found
	//   only within a fixed number of blocks back from the head, which is a different
	//   amount of TIME on every chain. A payment deposited and settled before that reach
	//   is not found.
	// - A batched settlement is read for at most 1024 entries, so a payment further into
	//   a larger batch is not found by its event. With its deposit anchored this answers
	//   503. After the deadline the destination scan above names it on `eip155:*` and
	//   `tron:*` when it can read the destination, and otherwise it stays 503.
	//
	// This is deliberate rather than provisional: a weaker label is always safe here
	// and a wrong one is not, so the lookup reports what it can prove and falls back
	// otherwise. Build on the presence of these codes as a refusal, never on their
	// absence, and never on the label being final.
	//
	// `DEPOSIT_NOT_HELD` reports that there is nothing here to refund and the history
	// could NOT be established: the fulfillment deadline has passed, every read completed,
	// and neither a deposit nor a settlement of this payment was found within the reach
	// above. A deposit veri-fill has found never answers it. It covers a deposit that never
	// existed and one deposited and settled outside the reach. Before the deadline the same
	// finding answers 503. It is terminal and is NOT a statement that the payment failed. A
	// consumer MUST NOT read it as any particular history. It is the COMMON fallback
	// for the cases above, not a rare one.
	//
	// No refund signature accompanies any of these, and a response carrying one MUST
	// NOT also carry `signature`.
	//
	// ENUM GROWTH IS EXPECTED HERE. A consumer MUST tolerate a value it does not know
	// and MUST treat an unrecognised code as terminal, because every code in this set
	// is. A consumer that rejects the response outright on an unknown value turns a
	// terminal answer into a retry loop that ends in a failed refund.
	Error *RefundSignatureResponseError `json:"error,omitempty"`

	// FulfillmentVerdict A signed attestation of what this service observed on the destination chain, so a
	// consumer can act on the signature instead of repeating the chain read.
	//
	// Distinct from the escrow signature alongside it. The response root's `signature` is
	// an EIP-712 release witness consumed by the escrow contract on the SOURCE chain; it
	// binds escrow members, carries neither a block nor a transaction, and its typehash is
	// fixed by deployed contracts. This object attests DESTINATION-chain observations and
	// is consumed offchain only. Two signatures over two structs, by two different keys in
	// a cross-chain-family corridor, and neither substitutes for the other.
	//
	// Also distinct from the fulfillment confirmation a settler reports about its own
	// transaction. That is a self-report by the party that sent it; this is a third-party
	// attestation by the verifier. Same nouns, different trust level - do not treat one as
	// the other.
	//
	// THEIR MEMBER ENCODINGS DIVERGE ON PURPOSE, and the divergence is recorded here so it
	// is not read as drift. That report is consumed as data and may spell a value however
	// its own schema allows; every member here is length-prefixed into signed bytes, so each
	// admits exactly ONE spelling and a producer normalises to it. Reconciling the two to a
	// shared encoding would either widen this object, giving a member several legal
	// preimages, or narrow a report that has no signature to protect. A consumer holding both
	// MUST normalise the report to this object's forms before comparing them, and MUST NOT
	// conclude a mismatch from a difference in spelling alone.
	//
	// WHAT A NEGATIVE DOES NOT ESTABLISH. A `not_delivered` outcome claims two things: that
	// no matching delivery had happened at `block_number`, and that one landing afterwards
	// could no longer count. The verifier observes the first. It cannot observe the second,
	// because what closes the window is a fulfillment deadline and NO CHAIN RECORDS ONE: it
	// is absent from the escrow, from the release witness, from the refund witness and from
	// the stored deposit, and reaches the verifier inside the awarded quote.
	//
	// This object therefore carries NO expiry and NO deadline, and that is deliberate rather
	// than an omission. Signing the deadline would present a quote term as a chain
	// observation, which is the one thing every member here forbids, and two payments with
	// different deadlines would obtain two different signed answers over identical chain
	// state. An expiry would put a clock back into a contract whose whole point is that only
	// a verdict moves a payment to a terminal state.
	//
	// What follows for a consumer: A NEGATIVE IS A STATEMENT ABOUT `block_number` AND ABOUT
	// NOTHING LATER. It stays true about that block forever and MUST NOT be read as true of
	// any later point, so a consumer needing a statement about a later point asks again. And
	// because the window cannot be attested, a producer that cannot establish it has closed
	// MUST emit `undetermined` instead: a wrong `not_delivered` is terminal, a wrong
	// `undetermined` costs one more ask.
	//
	// WHAT THIS OBJECT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY, so that a reader does not take the
	// absences for oversights:
	//
	//   * A CONFIRMATION DEPTH. `block_number` and `block_hash` already let anyone with chain
	//     access establish depth for themselves, and the check the producer applied, a depth
	//     or the chain's finalized tag, is published per chain by GET /v1/finality-config
	//     rather than restated here. It also has no meaning on `solana:*`, where finality is a
	//     commitment level and not a count, so a required member would be unfillable on one
	//     of the three families.
	//   * A BLOCK TIMESTAMP. It would only be actionable next to a signed deadline to compare
	//     it against, and per the note above there is none. On its own it invites exactly the
	//     comparison against an unattested deadline that this object refuses to make.
	//   * A SOURCE-CHAIN FACT OF ANY KIND. Those belong to `DepositVerdict`, whose chain
	//     member, block members and signing key are all source-scoped. See that object.
	//
	// OPTIONAL, AND ONLY EVER COMPLETE. A response without a verdict is well-formed and
	// means no attestation is available: the service does not evaluate verdicts, or it
	// could not reach a signable conclusion. ABSENCE IS NOT AN OUTCOME. A consumer MUST
	// NOT read it as delivered, as not-delivered, or as a failure of the payment, and MUST
	// fall back to the root `verified` and `error` members.
	//
	// A verdict is signed only for a conclusion drawn from chain state, and the line that
	// decides whether a verdict exists at all is whether a block can be named. Read the
	// chain successfully and the answer is attestable even when it is not terminal, which
	// is why `outcome` carries `undetermined` alongside the two terminal values: the
	// verifier looked, at a named final block, and the window is still open. Fail to read
	// the chain and there is no block to anchor anything to, so no verdict is issued and
	// the case goes out unsigned through the root `error` as `VERIFICATION_ERROR`. A
	// consumer MUST NOT treat either of those as terminal: the payment stays pending and is
	// re-asked. NEITHER a client polling budget expiring, NOR an HTTP 5xx, NOR a network
	// error is a finding about the payment either, and a consumer MUST NOT derive one from
	// any of them.
	//
	// Do not read the presence of a verdict as a terminal answer, nor its absence as a
	// negative one. Both mistakes are available and the members below are written to make
	// neither easy.
	//
	// PRECEDENCE, AND ONLY ONCE VERIFIED. A verdict is attacker-supplied data until its
	// signature checks out, so presence alone confers nothing. A consumer MUST first complete
	// every step of HOW A THIRD PARTY VERIFIES below; a verdict that fails any step MUST be
	// treated as ABSENT, per ABSENCE IS NOT AN OUTCOME above, and never as a negative
	// finding. Once verified, a consumer MUST treat the verdict as authoritative over the root
	// `verified` and `error`, which predate it and cannot express a signed claim. On the
	// release response a verified verdict and the root members agree, in one direction:
	// `verified: true` implies `outcome: delivered`, and `outcome: not_delivered` exactly
	// when `error` is `FULFILLMENT_NOT_DELIVERED`. The refund response pairs them the other
	// way round, because there `verified: true` means a refund was signed: see
	// `RefundSignatureResponse.error`.
	//
	// DELIVERED DOES NOT IMPLY `verified`. `verified` reports whether a release signature was
	// issued, not whether the delivery happened, and `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` is exactly where the two
	// part: the recipient was paid, so `outcome` is `delivered`, and the delivery fell outside the
	// quote's fulfillment window, so `verified` is FALSE and no `signature` accompanies it. A
	// consumer MUST NOT treat that pairing as a contradiction, and MUST NOT read the false
	// `verified` as non-delivery. Enforced by the request handler; OpenAPI 3.0.3 expresses no
	// cross-member constraint. This service never refuses a delivery for being late, so it
	// does not produce that pairing.
	//
	// EVERY MEMBER IS REQUIRED AND MUST BE SERIALIZED, including when the value is the
	// empty string. A producer MUST NOT suppress a member via `omitempty` or an
	// equivalent, and a consumer MUST reject a verdict in which any member is absent or
	// null BEFORE deriving what was signed. An absent member is not an empty member: a
	// renderer that interpolates a missing value produces a preimage containing that
	// language's word for undefined, which is a silent verification failure on the
	// commonest path rather than a loud one.
	//
	// SIGNING SCHEME (normative). `signature` covers the members of this object, and what
	// it is computed over is DERIVED from them. It is NOT carried on the wire: a consumer
	// that verified a signature against bytes it was handed would have verified nothing,
	// because an attacker could pair those bytes with unrelated typed members. The form
	// follows the chain family, as `signature_scheme` names it.
	//
	// On `eip155:*` (`eip712-secp256k1`) it is EIP-712 typed data, with the domain given on
	// `VerdictSignatureScheme` and the primary type
	//
	//   `FulfillmentVerdict(string domain,bytes32 paymentId,string chainId,string outcome,uint256 blockNumber,bytes32 blockHash,string finalityPolicy,bytes32 transactionHash,address fulfiller)`
	//
	// Each field carries the member of the same meaning verbatim: `chainId` is the whole
	// CAIP-2 `chain_id` string and `blockNumber` the decimal `block_number`. An empty
	// `transaction_hash` is encoded as the zero bytes32 and an empty `fulfiller` as the
	// zero address. That is unambiguous because both members are populated exactly when
	// `outcome` is `delivered`, and a producer never spells a zero value explicitly. A
	// consumer MUST reject an eip155 verdict whose `transaction_hash` or `fulfiller`
	// spells the zero value, since it signs the same digest as an empty one. The primary
	// type names the attestation kind, so typed data for one kind never hashes to the
	// digest of another.
	//
	// On `tron:*` and `solana:*` it is a preimage. The preimage is UTF-8 and consists of
	// exactly TEN lines, each terminated by one LF (0x0A) including the tenth, so it
	// contains exactly ten 0x0A bytes and its final byte is 0x0A. 0x0D is never emitted.
	// The order is fixed and no other line may appear:
	//
	//   1. the literal `atum-verdict/fulfillment/1`
	//   2. `domain=<n>:<domain>`
	//   3. `payment_id=<n>:<payment_id>`
	//   4. `chain_id=<n>:<chain_id>`
	//   5. `outcome=<n>:<outcome>`
	//   6. `block_number=<n>:<block_number>`
	//   7. `block_hash=<n>:<block_hash>`
	//   8. `finality_policy=<n>:<finality_policy>`
	//   9. `transaction_hash=<n>:<transaction_hash>`
	//  10. `fulfiller=<n>:<fulfiller>`
	//
	// `<n>` is the LENGTH IN BYTES of the value that follows, in base-10 with no leading
	// zeros, and `<n>:` is present even when the value is empty, which renders as `=0:`
	// followed by nothing.
	//
	// THE PREIMAGE'S FIRST LINE NAMES THE ATTESTATION KIND, not merely the format version,
	// and that is load-bearing rather than descriptive. A key this service holds for a chain
	// can sign every kind of attestation for that chain. Two kinds sharing a first line would
	// share a signing domain, and where their member lists agreed a signature over one would
	// satisfy a verifier of the other. A producer
	// MUST emit this exact first line and a consumer MUST reject a preimage that does not
	// begin with it, rather than accepting any line that merely looks like a verdict tag.
	//
	// The framing is what makes the rendering injective, and it is worth being precise about
	// why. The nine keys are fixed and in fixed order, so no key label is caller-chosen, and
	// each `<n>` is digits-only and therefore self-delimited by the first `:` after the key.
	// A value may consequently contain a `:` or begin with digits without being absorbed into
	// a neighbour. Under a bare `key=value` framing, by contrast, a value carrying an LF can
	// reproduce a following line, and two verdicts differing in `chain_id` can render to
	// identical bytes so that one signature authenticates both. The byte rejections below
	// would also stop that particular pair, since it needs an LF on both sides; the framing
	// is what removes the class rather than the instance, and it does so whether or not any
	// validator runs. That matters because the patterns in this schema are documentary -
	// neither the Go nor the TypeScript bindings validate them at runtime.
	//
	// Values are used exactly as they appear on the wire, byte for byte, with no case
	// folding, re-encoding or address normalization. Each member below admits exactly one
	// encoding per chain family for that reason: a consumer that normalizes a value before
	// deriving the typed data or preimage will fail to verify. `signature` and
	// `signature_scheme` are the envelope and are signed under neither form.
	//
	// EVERY MEMBER IS PRINTABLE ASCII, 0x20 to 0x7E inclusive, and a producer or consumer
	// MUST reject any value carrying a byte outside that range before rendering. This is not
	// tidiness: `<n>` counts BYTES, which Go's `len` gives directly but which a naive
	// JavaScript renderer using `.length` does not, since that counts UTF-16 code units. A
	// single non-ASCII byte therefore makes two good-faith implementations sign different
	// preimages for the same verdict, and a homoglyph in a hostname is a realistic
	// misconfiguration rather than a contrived input. Restricting the range removes the
	// divergence instead of asking every implementation to remember it. The framing bytes
	// 0x0A and 0x3D MUST likewise be rejected.
	//
	// Do NOT delegate these checks to regex anchoring: `$` in Python's `re` and in PCRE
	// matches before a trailing newline, so `re.match` and `preg_match` accept a value ending
	// in LF that the pattern is written to forbid. Use `re.fullmatch`, `\A...\z`, or an
	// explicit byte scan.
	//
	// HOW A THIRD PARTY VERIFIES, with no chain access, in order, failing closed at the
	// first step that does not hold:
	//   1. Reject the verdict unless every member is present and matches its pattern.
	//   2. Reject unless `payment_id` is the payment this consumer expects. A SIGNATURE
	//      PROVES WHAT WAS ATTESTED, NEVER WHICH QUESTION IT ANSWERS: without this step a
	//      genuine verdict about another payment passes every remaining step, and one
	//      payment's delivery marks another complete.
	//   3. Reject unless `chain_id` is the destination chain this consumer asked about.
	//      Without this step the verdict chooses its own trust anchor, because step 7
	//      fetches a key for whatever chain the verdict names.
	//   4. Reject unless `signature_scheme` matches the namespace of `chain_id`, and
	//      unless the length of `signature` matches `signature_scheme`.
	//   5. Reject unless `domain` equals `did:web:` followed by the lowercased host, without a
	//      port, of the fulfillment verifier endpoint the payment names. `domain` is a claim
	//      by the signer, not a fact - it is checked against the payment, never trusted as
	//      read. Without this step a verdict issued by one deployment verifies as a verdict
	//      from another whenever they derive from a shared signing backend.
	//   6. Derive what was signed from the members of THIS object: the typed data for
	//      `eip712-secp256k1`, the preimage for the other schemes.
	//   7. key = GET /v1/public-keys/{chain_id} -> PublicKeyResponse.address, asked of the
	//      fulfillment verifier endpoint the payment names.
	//   8. Verify per `signature_scheme`, and treat an unrecognised scheme as a rejection
	//      rather than falling through to a default.
	//
	// THE PAYMENT NAMES ITS VERIFIER. A consumer trusts the verifier the payment names and
	// keeps no list of its own. Deciding which verifiers are trusted belongs to the settlement operator:
	// a settlement operator MUST NOT submit a quote for a payment naming a verifier it does not trust.
	//
	// STEPS 2 AND 3 COMPARE AGAINST THE FORM THIS SCHEMA REQUIRES, NOT AGAINST THE VALUES THE
	// CONSUMER HOLDS. Both members are narrower here than the values a consumer holds:
	// `payment_id` than the payment id the consumer holds, and `chain_id` than the
	// destination chain as the quote spells it. Each is signed verbatim, length-prefixed
	// into the preimage or hashed into the typed data, and a value with several legal
	// spellings has several legal signed forms. A consumer that compared these verbatim
	// against the values it holds would reject every valid verdict where those values use
	// another legal spelling. Normalize the value asked about to the form named on each
	// member, then compare.
	//
	// Nothing in this object may be used in place of step 7. It deliberately carries no
	// signer address and no digest, because a member supplying its own trust anchor
	// verifies against itself and would accept a verdict signed by any key at all.
	FulfillmentVerdict *FulfillmentVerdict `json:"fulfillment_verdict,omitempty"`

	// MessageHash The hash of the message that was signed.
	// Used for signature verification. Format: Hex string without 0x prefix.
	MessageHash *string `json:"message_hash,omitempty"`

	// MessagePrehash The original message data before hashing, in JSON format.
	// Useful for understanding what was signed and debugging.
	MessagePrehash *string `json:"message_prehash,omitempty"`

	// PaymentId Hash of deposit data. Uniquely matches the given deposit
	PaymentId string `json:"payment_id"`

	// RefundTransactionHash The source-chain transaction that refunded the deposit: lowercase 0x-prefixed hex
	// on `eip155:*` and `tron:*`, a base58 signature on `solana:*`. Hex is 0x-prefixed on
	// every chain including `tron:*`, so that one value has one spelling.
	//
	// Present only with `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_REFUNDED`, and only when the refund was read
	// from the escrow's own `Refunded` event, or `RefundedMany` on `eip155:*` and
	// `tron:*`. Absent on every other answer, including a `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_REFUNDED`
	// named by a destination scan, which observes no refund transaction. It is unsigned:
	// a consumer that needs proof reads it on the source chain and confirms a `Refunded`
	// or `RefundedMany` event emitted by the escrow itself that names this deposit. The
	// transaction touching the escrow is not enough, since one `RefundedMany` covers many
	// deposits and another program can write a `Refunded`-shaped log line.
	RefundTransactionHash *string `json:"refund_transaction_hash,omitempty"`

	// Signature Cryptographic signature that authorizes the refund of escrowed funds.
	// This can be submitted to the escrow contract to claim the refund.
	// Format: 0x-prefixed lowercase hex (132 characters) on `eip155:*` and `tron:*`,
	// hex without a prefix (128 characters) on `solana:*`.
	Signature *string `json:"signature,omitempty"`

	// Verified Whether the refund signature was successfully generated
	Verified bool `json:"verified"`
}

// RefundSignatureResponseError Error code if signature generation failed.
//
// Bound to `deposit_verdict` and `fulfillment_verdict` when either is present. All
// four refusal codes below are TERMINAL as refusals: none is a reason to retry. The
// code itself is not final: a later ask can report a different one of the four as the
// deposit settles or its history moves out of reach.
//
// `fulfillment_verdict` attests the DESTINATION chain and is signed by the key
// served for its `chain_id`, never the source key behind the root `signature`. On
// this route its pairing is the inverse of the release response:
//
//   - `outcome: not_delivered` accompanies `verified: true` and the refund
//     `signature`, and names the final destination block the absence was proven to.
//   - `outcome: delivered` accompanies `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS`, and accompanies
//     `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_RELEASED` whenever a destination scan found the delivery, however
//     the release itself was established. The same code also appears WITHOUT a verdict
//     when no scan found one. A delivered verdict does not imply the delivery was on time. The release
//     route never refuses a delivery for being late, so on `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS`, where
//     the escrow still holds the deposit, the delivery named here is not stranded by its
//     timing.
//   - It is absent on every other answer, and on destinations this service cannot
//     attest yet (today only `eip155:*` destinations carry one). ABSENCE IS NOT AN
//     OUTCOME: a consumer MUST NOT read a missing verdict as either finding.
//
// A consumer MUST run every step of HOW A THIRD PARTY VERIFIES on
// `FulfillmentVerdict`, binding it to the payment and to the destination chain its
// own quote names, before acting on it.
//
// `deposit_verdict` accompanies `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS` and `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_RELEASED`
// when the deposit was found, with `outcome: held` anchored at the block its
// transaction was mined in. Today only `eip155:*` sources carry one. The deposit is found
// from `deposit_transaction_hash`, or from the escrow's own deposit events near the
// chain head. It is signed by the payment's `fulfillment_verifier.account`, the escrow
// releaser on the SOURCE chain. It is what lets a
// consumer that never saw the deposit record it, and it is absent on every other
// answer.
//
// `FULFILLMENT_EXISTS` reports that the payment WAS delivered on its destination
// chain, so a refund would pay twice. The escrow may still hold the deposit, which
// is exactly what a delivered-but-not-yet-released payment looks like, so a
// consumer MUST NOT infer from a live deposit that a refund is owed. It is also the
// answer when veri-fill already signed this payment's release and the delivery that
// release was granted on is still final on its destination chain.
//
// `RELEASE_ALREADY_SIGNED` reports that veri-fill already signed this payment's
// release, and that the delivery it was granted on could NOT be confirmed on its
// destination chain now: it was not found final there, or that chain cannot be
// re-read by this service. A signed verdict is final, so no refund will be signed,
// and veri-fill pages a person on it. It does NOT say the payment was delivered, and
// it does not say where the deposit is: the release may or may not have been
// submitted yet.
//
// `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_REFUNDED` and `DEPOSIT_ALREADY_RELEASED` report a settled
// history read from the escrow's own events, on `eip155:*`, `tron:*` and `solana:*`
// sources alike. When the escrow no longer holds the deposit, veri-fill first anchors
// the deposit: from `deposit_transaction_hash`, or from the escrow's deposit events in
// the most recent blocks on `eip155:*` and `tron:*`, or from the deposit account's
// transaction history on `solana:*`. On `eip155:*` and `tron:*` a deposit recorded
// under another quote is refused before any settlement is read; on `solana:*` the
// quote is checked against the settlement event's own when the event carries one.
// The escrow's `Refunded` and `Released` events, and `RefundedMany` and
// `ReleasedMany` on `eip155:*` and `tron:*`, are then scanned from the deposit
// onwards, however far back it lies. With no deposit anchored, the same recent
// blocks are still searched for a settlement of this payment, and a single
// settlement found there carries no quote to check, so neither code proves the
// caller's quote matched. A refund read this way carries its transaction in
// `refund_transaction_hash`.
//
// Both codes, and `INVALID_REQUEST`, are answered as soon as they are known, before
// the fulfillment deadline as well as after it. A history that cannot be read yet,
// because a read failed or the scan did not finish, answers 503, never a refusal.
//
// When the escrow's events cannot say and the deposit is anchored on an `eip155:*`
// or `tron:*` source, the answer after the deadline comes from a scan of the
// destination chain instead: `..._RELEASED` when it finds a delivery,
// `..._REFUNDED` when it finds none in the finalized history. A scan answer is a
// reading of the destination, not of the escrow: a delivery that has not finalized
// yet reads as none, and a delivery that landed after a refund reads as released.
// `..._RELEASED` means the recipient was paid.
//
// BOTH ARE BEST-EFFORT, AND THEIR ABSENCE PROVES NOTHING. A consumer MUST NOT assume
// a released payment always reports as released. Unless the destination chain
// resolves it as above, two gaps produce `DEPOSIT_NOT_HELD` for a payment that did in
// fact settle:
//
//   - Without `deposit_transaction_hash`, an `eip155:*` or `tron:*` deposit is found
//     only within a fixed number of blocks back from the head, which is a different
//     amount of TIME on every chain. A payment deposited and settled before that reach
//     is not found.
//   - A batched settlement is read for at most 1024 entries, so a payment further into
//     a larger batch is not found by its event. With its deposit anchored this answers
//     503. After the deadline the destination scan above names it on `eip155:*` and
//     `tron:*` when it can read the destination, and otherwise it stays 503.
//
// This is deliberate rather than provisional: a weaker label is always safe here
// and a wrong one is not, so the lookup reports what it can prove and falls back
// otherwise. Build on the presence of these codes as a refusal, never on their
// absence, and never on the label being final.
//
// `DEPOSIT_NOT_HELD` reports that there is nothing here to refund and the history
// could NOT be established: the fulfillment deadline has passed, every read completed,
// and neither a deposit nor a settlement of this payment was found within the reach
// above. A deposit veri-fill has found never answers it. It covers a deposit that never
// existed and one deposited and settled outside the reach. Before the deadline the same
// finding answers 503. It is terminal and is NOT a statement that the payment failed. A
// consumer MUST NOT read it as any particular history. It is the COMMON fallback
// for the cases above, not a rare one.
//
// No refund signature accompanies any of these, and a response carrying one MUST
// NOT also carry `signature`.
//
// ENUM GROWTH IS EXPECTED HERE. A consumer MUST tolerate a value it does not know
// and MUST treat an unrecognised code as terminal, because every code in this set
// is. A consumer that rejects the response outright on an unknown value turns a
// terminal answer into a retry loop that ends in a failed refund.
type RefundSignatureResponseError string

// ReserveLocation OPTIONAL. Where the reserve of this payment's deposit landed: for a direct deposit, the
// settler's own reserve, which it sends from the reserve it recorded. It is not what
// `deposit_transaction_hash` locates: that is the transaction that created the deposit
// (for a direct deposit, the payer's own). The three members travel together.
//
// A HINT, NEVER A FACT. Absent or wrong, the gate answers what it would have without it
// once its node has reached `block_number`. It cannot make veri-fill sign anything it would
// otherwise decline.
//
// HOW IT IS READ. veri-fill does not rely on the transaction or on a log index, because
// a settler may batch reserves and one transaction can then hold several
// `DepositReserved` logs. It asks its own node for the logs at `block_hash`, filtered by
// the Escrow it has configured for that chain, by topic0
// `DepositReserved(bytes32 indexed depositId, bytes32 quoteHash, address settler, uint256 amount, uint256 feeAmount)`
// and by this payment's deposit id as the indexed `depositId`, so other reserves in the
// same batch cannot be mistaken for this one.
//
// A log for this deposit id at `block_hash` is the reserve whichever transaction emitted it,
// so `transaction_hash` is not part of the match. `reserve()` has no caller check (it is
// authorised by the reserver's signature), so someone else can submit the settler's signed
// reserve first and the settler's own transaction then reverts; the deposit is reserved all
// the same. veri-fill records a `transaction_hash` that differs from the log's, ignoring
// case and the `0x` prefix, and does not act on it.
//
// veri-fill reads the location only when the escrow's stored hash still reads this deposit
// as unreserved, which is after the settled-history checks (never deposited, released,
// refunded), so a failing read of the location cannot hold a payment that history already
// settles.
//
//   - If the node's head is below `block_number`, the node has not caught up with the
//     reserve, and the refund answers 503 (retry) rather than a refusal.
//   - If the head is at or above `block_number`, veri-fill also reads the block at that
//     number. It ignores the hint when the node positively reports a different hash
//     there (a reorg or a wrong hash), or reports that same hash there and the log query at
//     `block_hash`, answered for that same block, returns no log for this deposit id (a
//     wrong id). An error, or an unknown block or hash, is not evidence that the block or
//     the log is absent, and neither is a `[]` that cannot be tied to the block the node
//     confirmed: the refund answers 503 (retry).
//   - A `block_number` too far above the node's head is ignored. veri-fill sets that cap.
//   - A `block_number` far below the node's head is ignored as well. The hint exists for a
//     node that lags just after the reserve, and a node that is long past the block is not
//     lagging, so a block it can no longer serve does not hold the refund at 503. veri-fill
//     sets that distance too.
//   - Send it only when the source chain is `eip155:*`. `block_hash` is an EVM hash, so a
//     location of another family fails validation. veri-fill ignores a well-formed one on a
//     source chain that is not `eip155:*`.
type ReserveLocation struct {
	// BlockHash Hash of the block at `block_number`, 0x-prefixed hex.
	BlockHash string `json:"block_hash"`

	// BlockNumber Height of the block that holds `transaction_hash`, as a base-10 string.
	//
	// A STRING, not a JSON number, for the same reason as `FulfillmentVerdict.block_number`.
	BlockNumber string `json:"block_number"`

	// TransactionHash The transaction that carried the reserve.
	TransactionHash string `json:"transaction_hash"`
}

// ReserveSignatureValidityRequest One reserve signature to check: the reserver, the reserve digest it signed, the
// signature, and the escrow that will verify it at deposit.
type ReserveSignatureValidityRequest struct {
	// Account The reserver the deposit authorization names, whose signature the escrow checks. EVM
	// hex on `eip155:*`, and on `tron:*` base58check or hex, with or without the 0x41
	// prefix byte.
	Account string `json:"account"`

	// ChainId The source chain, using CAIP-2 format: `eip155:<decimal chain id>` or
	// `tron:<cluster>`. Any other namespace, or a chain this instance does not serve, is
	// answered with `unsupported_chain`.
	ChainId string `json:"chain_id"`

	// Digest The 32-byte reserve digest the reserver signed, `reserve_auth.message`.
	Digest string `json:"digest"`

	// Escrow The escrow the reserve digest is bound to (its EIP-712 or TIP-712
	// `verifyingContract`), in any address spelling the chain accepts. It selects one of the
	// escrows this service is configured with for `chain_id`: any other address is answered
	// with `unsupported_chain`, and a value that is not an address with `malformed`.
	Escrow string `json:"escrow"`

	// Signature `reserve_auth.signature`, passed to `isValidSignature` exactly as sent. At most
	// 65536 characters, the `0x` included; a longer one is answered `malformed`.
	Signature string `json:"signature"`
}

// SettlementReport The awarded settler's signed statement that it gave up on fulfilling this payment: a
// SettlementResult declaration with phase FULFILLMENT and outcome ERROR, and the
// SettlementResultEnvelope holder VP whose proof.challenge is that declaration's content
// hash. Both members are the verbatim strings the settler sends on the settler session
// stream, so any holder of the report forwards them unchanged.
//
// A verified report removes the fulfillment deadline wait and the destination scan, so the
// refund is signed as soon as the deposit is live and anchored to the quote. veri-fill
// accepts it when the VP verifies for this environment's domain, the holder is the signer of
// `fulfillment_quote`, and the declared quote_hash (and, outside Solana, payment_id) match
// this request. It is ignored when the quote names the originator as the fulfill executor.
//
// AN UNVERIFIABLE REPORT IS NEVER A REFUSAL. A report that fails any check is treated
// exactly as an absent one, so the request is answered by the normal deadline path.
type SettlementReport struct {
	// Presentation The SettlementResultEnvelope holder VP JSON, exactly as signed.
	Presentation string `json:"presentation"`

	// SettlementResultJson The SettlementResult declaration JSON, exactly as signed.
	SettlementResultJson string `json:"settlement_result_json"`
}

// SignatureValidityPath The branch the verdict was reached on, in the verifying contract's rule: Permit2 for a
// depositor signature, the escrow for a reserve signature. `eoa`: the signer is recovered
// from the signature. `contract`: the account has code, and the verifying contract asks
// its `isValidSignature`. `none`: no branch was reached, because the request could not be
// evaluated or the account's code could not be read.
type SignatureValidityPath string

// SignatureValidityReason Why the signature is, or is not, valid. Only `verified` is valid. The verifying contract
// is Permit2 for a depositor signature and the escrow for a reserve signature.
//
// `verified`: the signature recovers to the account, or the account's `isValidSignature`
// returned the magic value when called from the verifying contract.
// `signer_mismatch`: the account has no code, and the signature does not recover to it with
// the verifying contract's spelling. The verifying contract would refuse it.
// `contract_signature_rejected`: the account has code, and `isValidSignature` reverted or
// returned anything but the magic value. The verifying contract would refuse it.
// `signature_unverifiable`: a chain read the rule needs failed. Nothing is known about the
// signature.
// `unsupported_chain`: `chain_id` is not an `eip155` or `tron` chain this instance serves,
// no Permit2 is configured for it, or a reserve check names an escrow it is not configured
// with.
// `malformed`: a member cannot be read as its type, or `signature` is longer than its
// `maxLength`.
//
// A consumer MUST refuse on `signer_mismatch`, `contract_signature_rejected` and
// `malformed`. The verifying contract refuses the first two. A `malformed` value breaks the
// spelling the signed message requires or the route's length bound, and is chosen by the
// submitter alone, so admitting it would let a submitter skip this check by respelling or
// padding a signature. `signature_unverifiable`, `unsupported_chain`, and any value this
// enum does not list are not refusals, so a consumer built against this set degrades safely
// if it grows.
type SignatureValidityReason string

// SignatureValidityRequest One depositor signature to check: the payment's source chain and account, and
// `signed_messages[0]` of its `sender_auth`, the message Permit2 checks. The Permit2 the
// contract path calls from is not a member: it comes from this service's configuration,
// never from the caller.
type SignatureValidityRequest struct {
	// Account The depositor, the permit owner Permit2 checks the signature for: the payment's
	// `source.account`. EVM hex on `eip155:*`, and on `tron:*` base58check or hex, with or
	// without the 0x41 prefix byte.
	Account string `json:"account"`

	// ChainId The source chain, using CAIP-2 format: `eip155:<decimal chain id>` or
	// `tron:<cluster>`. Any other namespace, or a chain this instance does not serve, is
	// answered with `unsupported_chain`.
	ChainId string `json:"chain_id"`

	// Digest The 32-byte digest the depositor signed, `signed_messages[0].message`.
	Digest string `json:"digest"`

	// Signature `signed_messages[0].signature`, passed to `isValidSignature` exactly as sent. A
	// contract account's signature is opaque: a multi-owner Safe's is 65 bytes per owner,
	// and one approved on chain is empty (`0x`). At most 65536 characters, the `0x`
	// included, which is 32767 bytes; a longer one is answered `malformed`.
	Signature string `json:"signature"`
}

// SignatureValidityResponse The verdict on one signature: a depositor signature (`POST /v1/signature-validity`) or a
// reserve signature (`POST /v1/reserve-signature-validity`).
type SignatureValidityResponse struct {
	// BlockNumber Height of the block of the last chain read the verdict used, as a base-10 string:
	// the `isValidSignature` call when one was made, otherwise the code read. The two
	// reads are separate and each is at the latest block, so the code read can be earlier.
	// Absent when the chain was not read (the signature recovers to the account,
	// `unsupported_chain`, `malformed`) and when the reader could not report the block. It
	// records what the answer is about, and is not a promise that the answer still holds.
	//
	// A STRING, not a JSON number, for the same reason as `FulfillmentVerdict.block_number`.
	BlockNumber *string `json:"block_number,omitempty"`

	// Path The branch the verdict was reached on, in the verifying contract's rule: Permit2 for a
	// depositor signature, the escrow for a reserve signature. `eoa`: the signer is recovered
	// from the signature. `contract`: the account has code, and the verifying contract asks
	// its `isValidSignature`. `none`: no branch was reached, because the request could not be
	// evaluated or the account's code could not be read.
	Path SignatureValidityPath `json:"path"`

	// Reason Why the signature is, or is not, valid. Only `verified` is valid. The verifying contract
	// is Permit2 for a depositor signature and the escrow for a reserve signature.
	//
	// `verified`: the signature recovers to the account, or the account's `isValidSignature`
	// returned the magic value when called from the verifying contract.
	// `signer_mismatch`: the account has no code, and the signature does not recover to it with
	// the verifying contract's spelling. The verifying contract would refuse it.
	// `contract_signature_rejected`: the account has code, and `isValidSignature` reverted or
	// returned anything but the magic value. The verifying contract would refuse it.
	// `signature_unverifiable`: a chain read the rule needs failed. Nothing is known about the
	// signature.
	// `unsupported_chain`: `chain_id` is not an `eip155` or `tron` chain this instance serves,
	// no Permit2 is configured for it, or a reserve check names an escrow it is not configured
	// with.
	// `malformed`: a member cannot be read as its type, or `signature` is longer than its
	// `maxLength`.
	//
	// A consumer MUST refuse on `signer_mismatch`, `contract_signature_rejected` and
	// `malformed`. The verifying contract refuses the first two. A `malformed` value breaks the
	// spelling the signed message requires or the route's length bound, and is chosen by the
	// submitter alone, so admitting it would let a submitter skip this check by respelling or
	// padding a signature. `signature_unverifiable`, `unsupported_chain`, and any value this
	// enum does not list are not refusals, so a consumer built against this set degrades safely
	// if it grows.
	Reason SignatureValidityReason `json:"reason"`

	// Valid True exactly when `reason` is `verified`.
	Valid bool `json:"valid"`
}

// SignedMessage The EIP-3009 authorization carried on the fulfillment authorization.
// message is the hash that was signed, and FulfillmentAuthorized.authorizationHash
// must equal it. veri-fill does not recover the signature.
type SignedMessage struct {
	// Message The data that was signed. Format depends on the blockchain:
	// - EVM: Hex-encoded message hash (with 0x prefix)
	// - Solana: Hex-encoded message hash (with 0x prefix)
	// - Tron: Hex-encoded message hash (possibly without 0x prefix)
	Message string `json:"message"`

	// MessagePrehash Optional: If 'message' contains a hash, this field contains the original data before it was hashed. Useful for verification and debugging.
	MessagePrehash *string `json:"message_prehash,omitempty"`

	// Payload Optional chain-specific payload containing additional signing context (e.g., delegate_signer for Solana). Preserved through the pipeline for downstream consumers.
	Payload *map[string]interface{} `json:"payload,omitempty"`

	// Signature The cryptographic signature proving you authorized this message. Hex-encoded with a 0x prefix on every supported chain. The signer's own public key is base58 on Solana, but the signature itself is not.
	Signature string `json:"signature"`
}

// VerdictSignatureScheme The complete signing rule for `signature`, named rather than inferred so that an
// unrecognised value fails closed at the signature check instead of falling into a
// consumer's default branch. It names the whole rule and not merely the curve,
// because the curve was never the ambiguous part: several mutually incompatible
// secp256k1 message conventions exist in practice and all of them produce a
// 130-character signature.
//
// The mapping is a closed bijection on the `chain_id` namespace - `eip155` to
// `eip712-secp256k1`, `tron` to `tip191-secp256k1`, `solana` to `ed25519-raw` - and
// a consumer MUST reject a verdict whose scheme does not match its namespace.
//
// `eip712-secp256k1`: 65 bytes, `r || s || v`. Recover the signer over the EIP-712
// digest `keccak256(0x19 || 0x01 || domainSeparator || hashStruct(message))` of the
// typed data defined under SIGNING SCHEME on `FulfillmentVerdict` and
// `DepositVerdict`. The domain type is
//
//	`EIP712Domain(string name,string version,uint256 chainId)`
//
// with name `Atum Verdict`, version `1` and chainId the decimal reference of the
// verdict's `chain_id`, and NO `verifyingContract`. A verdict is kept apart from the
// escrow witnesses the same key signs by its domain name and its type hashes, which no
// witness shares. A signing policy pins verdicts by `primary_type` together with
// `domain.name == "Atum Verdict"`.
//
// The typed data is signer input only. The verdict object is the canonical form: the
// message spells an empty member as an explicit zero, and that zero never appears on
// the wire. Typed data or a digest can be derived from any verdict, so a producer MUST
// validate the verdict against every member's pattern and the outcome rule before
// signing.
//
// `tip191-secp256k1`: 65 bytes, `r || s || v`. Recover the signer over
// `keccak256("\x19TRON Signed Message:\n" || decimal(byteLength(preimage)) ||
// preimage)`. Note the length is in BYTES; the preimage is all-ASCII so a
// code-unit length coincides, but only because every member's pattern is ASCII.
//
// `ed25519-raw`: 64 bytes, verified over the preimage BYTES with no hashing step.
//
// For both secp256k1 schemes: `s` MUST be low-s (`s <= n/2`) and a high-s
// signature MUST be rejected. `v` MUST be normalized on the verify side - subtract
// 27 if the final byte is at least 27, then require 0 or 1 - because both the
// `{0,1}` and `{27,28}` forms occur in practice from different signing backends and
// both recover the same signer. Compare the recovered 20 BYTES to the bytes decoded
// from the served address; do not compare address strings. Validate the served
// address separately: for `eip155:*` accept all-lowercase, all-uppercase, or a
// correctly checksummed EIP-55 string and reject a mixed-case string whose EIP-55
// checksum is wrong, since that checksum is the only integrity protection on the
// value the verifier fetched. For `tron:*` base58-decode the served address,
// require exactly 25 bytes, verify that bytes 21 to 25 are the first four bytes of
// `sha256(sha256(bytes 0 to 21))`, require byte 0 to be 0x41, and compare bytes 1
// to 21 - plain base58 yields 25 bytes, not 21, so "strip the 0x41" must not be
// read as "take everything after byte 0".
//
// For `ed25519-raw`: base58-decode the served address and require EXACTLY 32 bytes
// before verifying, because a short public key makes some implementations panic
// rather than return false. The signature MUST be canonical per RFC 8032 section
// 5.1.7 - reading `signature[32:64]` as a little-endian integer `S`, reject unless
// `S < L` where `L = 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493` - and the
// public key MUST be rejected if small-order or non-canonically encoded. This check
// is normative and MUST be performed explicitly: widely used Solana libraries omit
// it while other implementations enforce it, so the same verdict, key and signature
// verify in one and fail in the other, and any valid signature can be malleated
// into a second distinct signature over the same verdict.
//
// Domain separation for `ed25519-raw` rests entirely on the preimage's first line,
// because unlike the two secp256k1 schemes it adds no prefix or typed-data domain of
// its own. That is sufficient against any payload that cannot begin with those bytes,
// and a key this service holds for a chain also signs escrow witnesses for that chain
// when it is the source. A signer MUST therefore never raw-sign a
// payload whose leading bytes are any `atum-verdict/<kind>/<version>` line followed by
// LF for any purpose other than an attestation of that kind.
type VerdictSignatureScheme string

// VerificationDetails Detailed information about the verification process
type VerificationDetails struct {
	// AmountMatches Whether the amount matches expectations
	AmountMatches *bool `json:"amount_matches,omitempty"`

	// BlockConfirmed Whether the block is confirmed
	BlockConfirmed *bool `json:"block_confirmed,omitempty"`

	// EventFound Whether the expected event was found
	EventFound *bool `json:"event_found,omitempty"`

	// RecipientMatches Whether the recipient matches expectations
	RecipientMatches *bool `json:"recipient_matches,omitempty"`

	// SettlerMatches Whether the settler matches expectations
	SettlerMatches *bool `json:"settler_matches,omitempty"`

	// TokenMatches Whether the token matches expectations
	TokenMatches *bool `json:"token_matches,omitempty"`

	// TransactionFound Whether the transaction was found on chain
	TransactionFound *bool `json:"transaction_found,omitempty"`
}

// VerifyFulfillmentRequest A release request names the fulfill transaction and the awarded quote the payment
// was settled under. A quote is required in every accepted request, sent
// either as `fulfillment_quote` or inside `fulfillment_authorization` as
// `fulfillment_quote_json`. Exactly one of the two is sent, and a request with neither
// or both is refused. veri-fill enforces this rule, not the schema, so generated clients
// type both fields as optional.
//
// veri-fill takes the delivery terms (recipient, destination token and amount, and the
// fulfillment deadline), the source and destination chains, the settler, the depositor,
// the source token and the source amount from the verified quote and its embedded
// payment request, and the escrow and fulfillment contracts from its own per-chain configuration. None of
// them is accepted from the caller, so a caller cannot choose the delivery terms,
// chains or contracts the release signature binds. On EVM and Tron sources the release
// witness releaser is veri-fill's own signing address for the source chain.
//
// veri-fill derives the payment id, and on EVM and Tron sources the reserver, from the
// `sender_auth` the quote's embedded payment request carries, or from the caller's
// `sender_auth` when it carries none. The release is refused unless the escrow's stored
// deposit for that payment id matches, so the stored deposit decides.
